1. Original Problem Statement and Objectives
1.1 The Core Problem
In India, over 60% to 70% of coarse cereals, pulses (dal), and oilseeds are cultivated on rainfed agricultural land lacking perennial canal irrigation. When global climate anomalies occur—specifically the warming of sea surface temperatures in the equatorial Pacific Ocean known as El Niño—atmospheric circulation cells (Walker Circulation) shift, suppressing the Indian Southwest Monsoon (June to September).
This initiates a cascading vulnerability pipeline:
Rainfall Deficit & Dry Spells: Rainfed Kharif crops (Tur/Arhar, Moong, Urad, Soyabean, Bajra, Rainfed Paddy) suffer terminal moisture stress, flower drop, and yield reductions up to 25%.
Agricultural Wage Contraction: Landless agricultural laborers and smallholders experience an immediate collapse in farm employment days and seasonal household earnings.
Wholesale Food Inflation: Pulse prices surge 20% to 40% in wholesale mandis and retail village markets.
Dietary Dilution ("Hidden Hunger"): Low-income households spend >60% of their income on food. Facing rising prices, families cut high-protein lentils, dairy, and eggs, substituting them with cheap, calorie-dense starches (plain rice or broken wheat).
Rapid Pediatric Deterioration: Young children (0–59 months) rapidly deplete muscle mass and subcutaneous fat, sliding from normal nutritional status into Moderate Acute Malnutrition (MAM) or life-threatening Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM) within 6 to 12 weeks.
1.2 The Surveillance & Action Gap
Existing Government Data (NFHS): National Family Health Surveys publish retrospective cross-sectional surveys every 4 to 5 years. They cannot act as an early-warning system during an active drought season.
Clinical Health Trackers: Traditional pediatric growth apps assess child metrics in an isolated vacuum, completely ignoring local climate shocks, drought declarations, staple pulse inflation, or household ration access.
Grassroots Usability Gap: Scientific climate models and medical anthropometric guidelines (WHO Z-scores) are locked behind dense statistical jargon that parents, Anganwadi workers, and village panchayats cannot easily interpret.
1.3 Project Objectives
Primary Objective: Deliver an accessible, scientifically grounded web platform that maps macro-level El Niño climate shocks down to local child nutritional vulnerability across Indian states, providing real-time WHO anthropometric screening and personalized dietary/clinical action plans.
Specific Goals:
