9. Data Integrity, Limitations, and Unresolved Questions
9.1 Documented Assumptions in the Codebase
Linear Interpolation of WHO Knots: Age-based median and standard deviations are linearly interpolated between 13 discrete age knots (
) rather than calculating continuous cubic splines.
Allometric Power Law for WHZ: Expected weight-for-height is approximated using 
 (
) with linear standard deviation scaling. While matching WHO tables within 
 across the 
 range, it is an empirical approximation rather than the official WHO Box-Cox power exponential (
) table lookup.
Static State NFHS-5 Baselines: The 14 state profiles in indiaData.ts use static cross-sectional values from the NFHS-5 survey (2019–21). They do not currently pull live API telemetry from the Poshan Tracker or IMD Mausam portals.
Rainfed Sensitivity Scaling: The state rainfall sensitivity score (
) is an engineered composite score based on historical monsoon deviation records rather than an automated satellite soil moisture feed.
9.2 Known Unresolved Elements from Past Iterations
22 States & UTs Not Fully Profiled: The code defines 14 primary agrarian states. While the architecture covers all 6 zones, 22 smaller states and Union Territories (e.g., Goa, Sikkim, Ladakh, Andaman) fall back to default or unlisted parameters.
Offline PWA Caching: While calculations run 
 client-side, the app has not yet registered a progressive web app service worker (manifest.json / Workbox) for field use without internet connectivity.
Multilingual Localization: Field workers in rural Anganwadis operate primarily in Hindi, Marathi, Telugu, Kannada, Bengali, and Odia. The interface is currently in English.
